<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
          xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

  <!--
    All-files access. SnapZap has to reach folders the system Photos library does not index
    (docs/ANDROID-PORT-PLAN.md §1/§3) — that is the entire reason MediaStore/ContentResolver was
    ruled out, and it is what lets SnapZap.Core's Scanner run on raw paths completely unmodified.

    Google Play gates this permission hard. That is fine here and only here: SnapZap is sideloaded
    for personal use and does not go through Play review. Revisit this the moment store
    distribution is on the table, because it is the one decision that would block it.

    tools:ignore silences the lint rule that (correctly, for a store app) flags it.
  -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
                   tools:ignore="ScopedStorage" />

  <!--
    Pre-API-30 read access. Kept only so the manifest is honest about intent; the app's floor is
    API 30 (SupportedOSPlatformVersion in the csproj) precisely because MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE
    and its request intent are API 30+.
  -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
                   android:maxSdkVersion="32" />

  <!--
    A long scan is minutes of continuous decoding and hashing. Android freezes or kills a
    backgrounded process doing that — when the user switches apps, or the screen sleeps — and from
    their side the app just "randomly stops". A foreground service is the only supported way to say
    the work was asked for and should be allowed to finish. dataSync is its type: this is bulk local
    file processing, not media playback or location.

    POST_NOTIFICATIONS is runtime-requested on API 33+. If it is refused the work still runs and is
    still protected — only the progress read-out is lost — so it is asked for, never insisted on.
  -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_DATA_SYNC" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

  <!--
    Note what is NOT here: android.permission.INTERNET, and any networkSecurityConfig permitting
    cleartext to 127.0.0.1. The port plan §3 required both, because its architecture ran Kestrel
    in-process and pointed a WebView at loopback. That architecture is dead (ANDROID-PORT-ACS
    §1.5), and the native UI that replaced it makes no network requests of any kind. An app that
    reads your entire photo library and cannot talk to the network is a materially better thing to
    hand someone — do not add INTERNET back without a specific reason.
  -->

  <!--
    The launcher icon. Generated from assets/icon/snapzap-256.png — the same source art
    scripts/make-icons.py uses for the desktop favicon and the .exe icon — so the phone icon
    cannot drift from the one on the desktop. Regenerate from that source, never edit the
    mipmaps directly.
  -->
  <application android:label="SnapZap"
               android:icon="@mipmap/appicon"
               android:allowBackup="false" />
</manifest>
